Modular dungeon tiles
---------------------

Source: "Modular Dungeon Kit" by Kevin Barany / SigilsVault
(sigilsvault.itch.io, sigilsvault.gumroad.com). A low-poly dark-fantasy
kit of walls, floors, ceilings, pillars and props on a 1-metre grid,
trim-sheet textured.
License: CC0 1.0 (public domain dedication). Credit is appreciated but
not required. The full terms are in the kit's LICENSE.txt.

The pieces under content/rooms are the subset baked into the game: a
floor, a wall, a ceiling, and a pillar, converted from the kit GLBs to
shared-texture meshes that sample the kit's handpainted (mossy) trim
sheet. They are the first modular-tile set, used by the chamber in
content/rooms/chamber.room.

Modular cathedral tiles
-----------------------

Source: "Modular Cathedral Kit" by Kevin Barany / SigilsVault
(sigilsvault.itch.io). A low-poly gothic cathedral kit of walls,
floors, vaulted ceilings, columns and props on a 1-metre grid,
trim-sheet textured, with a separate stained-glass atlas.
License: CC0 1.0 (public domain dedication). Credit is appreciated but
not required. The full terms are in the kit's LICENSE.txt.

The cath_* pieces under content/rooms/pieces are the subset baked into
the game (floor, wall, vaulted ceiling, column), converted from the kit
GLBs to shared-texture meshes that sample the kit's cathedral trim
sheet. Kept at the kit's native grand scale for the cathedral theme,
and reused for the town temple from a warm-tinted bake of the same
trim sheet.

Spell catalog
-------------

Source: the AX&X spell lists (the author's own two-page OSR ruleset, a refit
of the classic 1st Edition advanced rules). content/spells.txt is imported
from that project's spells.csv: the Cleric, Druid, and Magic-User lists across
six spell levels, with each spell's terse effect prose kept as the behaviour.
The spell names follow the long-standing lineage AX&X refits.

The crawler reads the prose as the real behaviour and maps each spell's damage
type onto its own five elements thematically, so combat that wires a spell up
approximates the intent rather than reproducing exact tabletop mechanics.

Bundled libraries (macOS)
-------------------------

Source: Simple DirectMedia Layer 2 (SDL2) by Sam Lantinga and the SDL
contributors, https://www.libsdl.org/.
License: zlib license (free to use and redistribute, including commercially;
an acknowledgement is appreciated but not required).

On the other targets the game loads the system or distribution SDL2 at
runtime. The macOS .app has no system SDL2 to find, so it bundles the
official arm64 SDL2 dylib in Contents/Frameworks and loads it from there.
The dylib is supplied at build time and is not stored in this repository.
